Z||000000||3fifty||11-13-2001||11:22 PM||||My 85 GT is lowered 1.5 and has 245/45 16's on the rear. Body is about 1 1/4" above the rear tires. What strut will be stiff enough<BR>as not to let the body hit after the 350 goes in? Koni's are to high (389.00) for struts only. KYB G2's? Coilover kit from WeastCoast Fiero? <P>Thanks,<BR>     350 soon!!!!!||||reg||1
Z||000001||FieroMGDriver||11-14-2001||12:12 AM||||Take a look at Winston Ojeda's website.  He did coil overs and he has an LT1. <A HREF="http://www.fierolt1.com/coilsnews.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fierolt1.com/coilsnews.htm</A> ||||reg||1
Z||000002||California Kid||11-14-2001||10:09 AM||||You might think the Koni's are priced too high, but you get what you pay for!!! Mine were put on the car in 1993, have clocked approx 30,000 miles of hard driving and there are no problems to date. Car still handles beyond comprehension!!!<BR><P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Signature_7.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000003||1FST2M6||11-14-2001||02:14 PM||||Koni.. <A HREF="http://www.shox.com" TARGET=_blank>www.shox.com</A>  i got (on my SE) the 4 koni and eibachs for $631 shipped.  or ask Doug Greenup at <A HREF="http://www.wheel.com" TARGET=_blank>www.wheel.com</A>  they too have Koni. I've got bilsteins now and kick myself for not getting the koni again. ||||reg||1
Z||000004||jelly2m8||11-14-2001||05:51 PM||||I just left the old stock ones on My V8. Looks to sit the same height to me. Tires are stock sized 215-60-14's<P>I know, poor quality pics.<P>Dear santa, for christmas I would like a digital camera......;p<P> <IMG SRC="http://users.eastlink.ca/~acook1/tips%201a.jpg"> <BR> <IMG SRC="http://users.eastlink.ca/~acook1/beast.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000005||JB||11-14-2001||10:30 PM||||Hey Jelly, While you are writing to Santa, You might want to ask him for a new set of rims and tires that are wide enough to handle the power of the v8 in the fiero!!!!!   P.S......... I'd like some stuff from him too!!!!||||reg||6
Z||000006||artherd||11-15-2001||02:55 AM||||Konis also are waranteed for LIFE...<P>Best!<BR>Ben.<P>(they can be a pain if you want to convert to  coilover rears in the future though. Seems they have a lip that won't accept the usual threaded-sleeve thing.)<BR><P>------------------<BR>      <IMG SRC="http://www.cartsys.com/fiero/sig1.jpeg">    <BR>Ben Cannon<BR>88 Formula, T-top, Metalic Red           &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;"Every Man Dies, not every man really Lives"<BR>88 Formula, Northstar, Silver                    &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;-Mel Gibson, "Braveheart"||||reg||3
Z||000007||lowCG||11-17-2001||11:34 AM||||The Bilsteins I put into some Citation strut housings have been working great for years now,the pistons are nearly the same dia. as the strut,and the stock bumpstops fit perfectly over the larger piston.The strut cartridges from a VW GolfII will work,and I carefully ground away about a 1/2" from the top of the shaft where the upper mounting goes for additional travel,making sure the internal bumpstop wasn't used.<BR>The larger piston will give you much more bearing area to handle the added torque of that motor.<BR>You can also turn the upper strut mount upside down to get more travel out of the rear suspension,you just have to provide a place for the spring to locate on the plate that's up in there.||||reg||1
